---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="AI For Good is a student-led organisation in Hong Kong, founded in June 2023: written recommendations to government, and free tutoring for underprivileged children."
>
  <section class="page-top">
    <div class="container prose">
      <p class="kicker">About</p>
      <h1>Two halves of one argument</h1>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 1. What AFG is -->
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container prose">
      <p class="lead">
        AI For Good is a student-led organisation in Hong Kong, founded in June
        2023. We do two things: we write recommendations on AI and economic
        policy and submit them to the Hong Kong government, and we teach free
        tutoring to underprivileged primary school children. Roughly twenty
        recommendations go to government each year; 350 children have been
        taught so far.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. Why both — the connective idea -->
  <section class="why">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Why we do both</h2>
      </div>
      <blockquote class="why-claim">
        AI policy written without seeing who it lands on is bad policy — and the
        children we teach are exactly who it lands on.
      </blockquote>
      <div class="prose why-body">
        <p>
          The two halves are not a coincidence of interests. Sitting in a
          classroom in Ho Man Tin and watching a child meet an AI tutor for the
          first time tells you something a consultation paper cannot: which
          children already have a tutor at home, which have a device, and which
          are about to be left further behind by a technology everyone is
          calling an equaliser.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is where most of our submissions come from. The teaching shows us
          the gap; the research works out whether it is a policy problem; the
          submission asks the government to close it. It also runs the other
          way — what we learn writing about education and labour policy changes
          what we think is worth teaching a ten-year-old. We would rather be the
          organisation that has been in the room than the one that has only read
          about it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. Origin -->
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>How it started</h2>
        <p>
          AI For Good was founded in June 2023 by <strong>Justin Lo</strong>,{' '}
          <strong>Ka Hei Ng</strong> and <strong>Oscar Zheng</strong>, and
          joined at a later stage by <strong>David Dong</strong> and{' '}
          <strong>Jackie Xue</strong>. It began outside school, as an
          independent think tank rather than anyone's club, and has since grown
          to 35+ members and expanded into partnerships with schools across
          Hong Kong — which is how the tutoring programme reaches the children
          it does.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Justin's own account, in the first person and left in his words.
           The voice shift from "we" to "I" is deliberate and attributed. -->
      <div class="founder-note">
        <div class="fn-part">
          <p class="fn-label">Why the policy work</p>
          <p>
            AI arrived in Hong Kong faster than the city could decide who it was
            for. I watched it show up in my school, then in my friends'
            internships, then in the government's plans, and the pattern was
            always the same: the people who already had the most access got it
            first. Nobody was choosing that. It was just what happened when
            nobody was asked to choose. I had spent two years learning how
            public money gets allocated and who ends up bearing the cost of a
            decision, and it seemed absurd to sit on that while the decisions
            were being made a few kilometres away. So we started writing to the
            people making them.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="fn-part">
          <p class="fn-label">Why the teaching</p>
          <p>
            This started as a group of us at school teaching each other AI,
            mostly by playing with it. We built things that didn't work and a
            few that did, and it was obvious how much you could get out of these
            tools once someone showed you the first step. Then it became obvious
            who wasn't getting shown. The children with the most to gain from AI
            were the least likely to ever be handed it, and that gap wasn't
            going to close on its own. So we took what we'd been doing for fun
            in a classroom and brought it to schools where nobody else was
            offering it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p class="fn-attrib">— Justin Lo, founder</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="origin-figure">
        <img
          src="/programs/team-outside-school.jpg"
          alt="Five AI For Good members outside Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School."
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>Outside Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School, where we teach.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. What we do — two blocks -->
  <section class="tint">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="mb">What we do</h2>
      <div class="two-blocks">
        <div class="block block-policy">
          <h3>Policy</h3>
          <p>
            We submit roughly twenty written recommendations to the Hong Kong
            government each year. We have filed to Policy Address consultations
            and Budget proposal feedback in every cycle since 2024, and we are
            currently submitting to the First Five-Year Plan for Economic and
            Social Development (2026–2030). The subjects follow from the work:
            taxation, AI governance, education, labour, and health. We argued
            for an inclusive AI programme in successive submissions; the
            2026–27 Budget allocated HK$50 million to AI for All.
          </p>
          <a class="link-quiet" href="/policy">Read the policy work →</a>
        </div>
        <div class="block block-teaching">
          <h3>Teaching</h3>
          <p>
            We run free tutoring for underprivileged primary students across
            seven schools in Hong Kong. Forty-three trained tutors teach four
            modules — mathematics, English, economics, and science — each
            pairing a school subject with AI as a free, patient learning tool.
            Three hundred and fifty children have been taught so far. Since
            2024, 86% of our students have moved up at least one class band.
          </p>
          <a class="link-quiet" href="/teaching">See the teaching →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. How we work -->
  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="mb">How we work</h2>
      <div class="two-blocks">
        <div class="method">
          <p class="method-label">Writing a proposal</p>
          <p>
            Member interviews, then research, then drafting. Every proposal
            states the case for, then the case against, then the
            recommendation — in that order, in every submission we file. If the
            case against wins, the proposal does not go out.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="method">
          <p class="method-label">Running a session</p>
          <p>
            Tutors are trained before they teach. They work from a written
            curriculum built around the four modules, so a child gets the same
            standard of session whichever school they are in and whichever tutor
            is in front of them.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. Impact — outcomes lead, inputs sit smaller -->
  <section class="impact">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="mb">Impact</h2>
      <div class="outcomes">
        <div class="outcome outcome-policy">
          <p class="outcome-figure">HK$50M</p>
          <p class="outcome-label">
            allocated to AI for All in the 2026–27 Budget — the inclusive AI
            programme we argued for
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="outcome outcome-teaching">
          <p class="outcome-figure">86%</p>
          <p class="outcome-label">
            of our students have moved up at least one class band since 2024
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="inputs">
        350 children taught · 7 schools · 43 tutors trained · 35+ members ·
        ~20 recommendations a year
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7. Partners -->
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>Partners</h2>
      <p>
        We teach with Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School, St. Barnabas' Home
        &amp; Society, Kennedy Town School, and 10% Organisation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 8. Where we're going -->
  <section class="section-tight ahead">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>What's next</h2>
      <ul class="ahead-list">
        <li>
          <span class="ahead-tag ahead-policy">Policy</span>
          We are submitting to the First Five-Year Plan for Economic and Social
          Development (2026–2030), on how AI and economic policy can widen
          rather than narrow opportunity.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="ahead-tag ahead-teaching">Teaching</span>
          We return to Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School next year, with the
          four modules and a new cohort of tutors.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 9. Get involved -->
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>Get involved</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Schools and organisations:</strong> if you work with
        underprivileged students and want tutors, we bring the curriculum, the
        tutors, and the equipment — <a href="/get-involved">get in touch</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Students:</strong> we have opened recruitment at Victoria
        Shanghai Academy for students interested in policy, economics, and AI,
        and we look for people who care more about the work than the title —{' '}
        <a href="/get-involved">tell us what you'd want to work on</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-top {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .mb {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  /* 2. Why both */
  .why {
    border-block: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .why-claim {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 2.8vw, 1.95rem);
    line-height: 1.35;
    max-width: 34ch;
    margin-block: 0.5rem 1.75rem;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    /* The one place the two inks meet, because the claim joins them */
    border-left: 4px solid var(--policy);
    border-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--policy), var(--teaching)) 1;
  }

  .why-body {
    color: var(--graphite);
  }

  /* 3. Origin — the founder's note, set apart from the institutional voice */
  .founder-note {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--policy);
    border-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--policy), var(--teaching)) 1;
  }

  .fn-part + .fn-part {
    margin-top: 1.4rem;
  }

  .fn-label {
    font-weight: 650;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    color: var(--graphite);
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }

  .fn-part p:last-child {
    font-size: 1.06rem;
  }

  .fn-attrib {
    margin-top: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--graphite);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .origin-figure {
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .origin-figure img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    aspect-ratio: 3 / 2;
    object-fit: cover;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .origin-figure figcaption {
    color: var(--graphite);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
  }

  /* 4 & 5. Two-column blocks */
  .tint {
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, #f6f3ec, var(--paper));
    border-block: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .two-blocks {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 2.5rem;
  }

  .block {
    border-top: 3px solid var(--rule);
    padding-top: 1.1rem;
  }

  .block h3 {
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }

  .block p {
    color: var(--graphite);
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }

  .block-policy {
    border-top-color: var(--policy);
  }

  .block-policy .link-quiet {
    color: var(--policy);
  }

  .block-teaching {
    border-top-color: var(--teaching);
  }

  .block-teaching .link-quiet {
    color: var(--teaching-deep);
  }

  .method-label {
    font-weight: 650;
    margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
  }

  .method p:last-child {
    color: var(--graphite);
  }

  /* 6. Impact */
  .impact {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .outcomes {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 2.5rem;
    max-width: 46rem;
  }

  .outcome-figure {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 6vw, 3.9rem);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .outcome-policy .outcome-figure {
    color: var(--policy);
  }

  .outcome-teaching .outcome-figure {
    color: var(--teaching-deep);
  }

  .outcome-label {
    color: var(--graphite);
    max-width: 24ch;
  }

  .inputs {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    padding-top: 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    color: var(--graphite);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  /* 8. What's next */
  .ahead {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .ahead-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .ahead-list li {
    padding-block: 0.9rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    color: var(--graphite);
  }

  .ahead-tag {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    font-weight: 650;
    margin-right: 0.6rem;
  }

  .ahead-policy {
    color: var(--policy);
  }

  .ahead-teaching {
    color: var(--teaching-deep);
  }

  @media (max-width: 52rem) {
    .two-blocks,
    .outcomes {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 1.75rem;
    }
  }
</style>
